The text of the report is given page by page; each page starts with its number, written [p. N]. The bibliography has been removed.

For each field, in the order above, give exactly one entry with its "code" (a multiple choice lists all its choices in that single entry):
- "reported": false when the report does not give this information; then leave the other keys empty;
- otherwise "reported": true and the value:
  - text: a short, faithful summary in the language of the report, or its exact words;
  - number: digits only (use a point for decimals);
  - boolean: "yes" or "no";
  - date: YYYY, YYYY-MM or YYYY-MM-DD;
  - single choice or hierarchical category: exactly one of the choices, copied exactly, in "value", and "selected" left empty; when several choices seem to apply, give the one choice that covers them if there is one (for example a choice naming both), otherwise the one that fits best;
  - multiple choice: the choices that apply, copied exactly, in "selected" ("value" empty); "selected" is used for no other type;
- "quote": the exact words of the text the value rests on, copied as they are written (original language, at most about 40 words, within one page if you can); never quote words that are not in the text, never paraphrase inside a quote;
- "page": the number N of the page the quote is on.

Fields with choices often ask you to classify the study (its focus, its design, the concepts it covers) rather than to find a sentence that names a choice. Classifying what the text says into the choices of the grid is what the reviewers expect: choose the choice whose wording and definition fit the study, from the parts of the report the definition points to (for example its aims, its methods or its measures), and quote the words that support it. "reported": false is only for a study whose text gives nothing to classify.

For a multiple choice, select a choice only when the study itself examines it: data collected, measured, analysed or reported in its findings. A choice mentioned only in the background, the theoretical framework or the discussion is not selected. Give all the choices of a multiple choice in one entry, with one quote: if they rest on different passages, quote the passage that supports most of them.

Values about the participants (numbers, ages, characteristics) describe the participants who belong to the population of the review question. When the report gives them separately for that population and for other groups (comparison groups, other conditions), give the values for the population of the review question.
